this question." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186115991 1071 "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $12$ defective bolts in a box containing $20$ bolts if the probability that an individual bolt is defective is $0.2.$\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player scores $20$ of $30$ penalties if the probability of scoring each penalty is $0.55.$\n\nIII. The probability of getting $9$ fives on $28$ rolls of a $6$-sided die." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186115991 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=30$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =30\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =16.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =13.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=28$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =28\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186116534 1064 "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an even exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^6 = 64\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x$.\n\nSince the exponent $(6)$ is even, there will be two real solutions (positive and negative):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{6} &= 64 \\\\\n\\sqrt[6]{x^{6}} &= ± \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\\nx &= ± \\sqrt[6]{2^{6}} \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2$.\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n> *When $n$ is even, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$ with $a \\geq 0$, we get two solutions (positive and negative):* $x = \\pm\\sqrt[n]{a}$\n\n**Watch out!** If $a<0$ and $n$ is even, equation $x^n = a$ has no solution since, in this case,\n\n$$\nx^n \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor any real $x$." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186116534 1064 "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an even exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^6 = 64?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince the exponent $(6)$ is even, there will be two real solutions (positive and negative):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{{x}^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is even, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$ with $a \\geq 0,$ we get two solutions (positive and negative):* \n\n$$\nx = \\pm\\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If $a<0$ and $n$ is even, equation  $x^n = a$ has no solution since, in this case, $x^n \\geq 0$ for any real $x.$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186116536 1064 "Suppose the random variable $X\\sim B(n,p)$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\nThere are certain situations where working with binomial random variables becomes difficult. For example, when the parameter $n$ is large, computing exact probabilities can become problematic due to the large numbers involved.\n\nUnder certain conditions, we can approximate the *discrete* random variable $X$ using a *continuous normal* random variable $Y$, given by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are calculated using the formulas for the mean and variance of the binomial random variable $X$:\n\n$$\n\\mu = np, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTo determine whether a given binomial distribution can be approximated using a normal distribution, we use the following rule of thumb:\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\nTo get a feel for how this works, let's consider a few examples of binomial distributions and their corresponding normal approximations:\n\n- First, consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.2$. In this case, it's straightforward to show that\n$\\mu = {\\color{blue}{1}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{0.8}}$,\nand\n$np = 1 < 5,\\qquad n(1-p) = 4 < 5$.\nSince $np < 5$, the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{0.8}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.2)$. The diagram below compares the two distributions. The yellow rectangles correspond to the values of the PMF of $B(5, 0.2)$, and the smooth blue curve is the PDF of the corresponding normal distribution.![](images/6a/6a67b3ad65bfedcd25b787ff15e72c575da1215ae47502ff11f97d40de407560.png)\n- Next, we consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.5$. In this case, it's straightforward to show that\n$\\mu = {\\color{blue}{2.5}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{1.25}}$,\nand\n$np = n(1-p) = 2.5 < 5$.\nSince $np = n(1-p) < 5$, the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{2.5}},{\\color{red}{1.25}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.5)$.![](images/0a/0a8f2d9ccd78ee885ea3bb8f2f00705f69c520f8ec0588411865399b6ecef046.png)\n- Finally, we consider the case where $n=20$ and $p=0.5$. In this case, it's straightforward to show that\n$\\mu = {\\color{blue}{10}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{5}}$,\nand\n$np = n(1-p) = 10 > 5$.\nSince $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$, the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{10}},{\\color{red}{5}})$ *is* a good approximation to $B(20, 0.5)$.![](images/9e/9efc7df7c423ed2d8d6cc70ef820de3cb235f644567a3fec0d0a0e501042e305.png)\n\nThe fact that we can approximate binomial random variables as normal when $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ is a consequence of the so-called **central limit theorem** (or CLT). We'll discuss this in more detail at the end of the lesson." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186116536 1064 "Suppose the random variable $X\\sim B(n,p)$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\nThere are certain situations where working with binomial random variables becomes difficult. For example, when the parameter $n$ is large, computing exact probabilities can become problematic due to the large numbers involved.\n\nUnder certain conditions, we can approximate the *discrete* random variable $X$ using a *continuous normal* random variable $Y,$ given by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are calculated using the formulas for the mean and variance of the binomial random variable $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mu = np, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTo determine whether a given binomial distribution can be approximated using a normal distribution, we use the following rule of thumb:\n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nTo get a feel for how this works, let's consider a few examples of binomial distributions and their corresponding normal approximations:\n\n- First, consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.2.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{1}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{0.8}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = 1 < 5,\\qquad n(1-p) = 4 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{0.8}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.2).$ \n\nThe diagram below compares the two distributions. The yellow rectangles correspond to the values of the PMF of $B(5, 0.2),$ and the smooth blue curve is the PDF of the corresponding normal distribution.\n\n![](images/6a/6a67b3ad65bfedcd25b787ff15e72c575da1215ae47502ff11f97d40de407560.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{2.5}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{1.25}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 2.5 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np = n(1-p) < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{2.5}},{\\color{red}{1.25}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/0a/0a8f2d9ccd78ee885ea3bb8f2f00705f69c520f8ec0588411865399b6ecef046.png)\n\n- Finally, we consider the case where $n=20$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{10}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{5}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 10 > 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{10}},{\\color{red}{5}})$ *is* a good approximation to $B(20, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/9e/9efc7df7c423ed2d8d6cc70ef820de3cb235f644567a3fec0d0a0e501042e305.png)\n\nThe fact that we can approximate binomial random variables as normal when $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ is a consequence of the so-called **central limit theorem** (or CLT). We'll discuss this in more detail at the end of the lesson." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186116542 1064 "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6)$. Let's estimate the value of\n\n$$\nP(X=33)\n$$\n\nusing the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, if\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$,\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nthat is,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p))\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnp &= 50 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 30 \\\\\n&> 5✓ \\\\\nn(1 - p) &= 50 \\cdot (1 - 0.6) \\\\\n&= 20 \\\\\n&> 5.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12)\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(32.5 \\le Y \\le 33.5) &= P(\\frac{32.5 - 30}{\\sqrt{12}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{33.5 - 30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(0.72 \\le Z \\le 1.01) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 1.01) - P(Z < 0.72) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 1.01) - P(Z \\le 0.72) \\\\\n&= Φ(1.01) - Φ(0.72)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(32.5 \\le Y \\le 33.5) &= Φ(1.01) - Φ(0.72) \\\\\n&= 0.8438 - 0.7642 \\\\\n&= 0.0796\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186116542 1064 "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6).$ Let's estimate the value of $P(X=33)$ using the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12).\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =P(\\frac{32.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{33.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.72\\le Z\\le 1.01) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z<0.72) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z\\le 0.72) \\\\  & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72) \\\\  & =0.8438-0.7642 \\\\  & =0.0796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796.\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186116543 1064 "In the last example, we approximated the binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim B(50,0.6)\n$$\n\nusing a normal random variable\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12)\n$$\n\nand we arrived at the approximation\n\n$$\nP(X=33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5) \\approx 0.0796\n$$\n\nIf you were to use a software package to evaluate this approximation, you might get an answer like\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5) \\approx 0.0791\n$$\n\nNotice that there is a difference of around $0.0005$ between the two answers.\n\nThere is a reason for this discrepancy. When we calculated that\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5) \\approx 0.0796\n$$\n\nwe rounded our $z$-values two decimal places and then used a lookup table to find $\\Phi(z)$ for those rounded values. As a result, we introduced some small rounding errors.\n\nTypically, the rounding errors are small, so we will continue to work with a table of values. However, it's worth bearing this in mind if you're using a software package or calculator to compute $\\Phi(z)$." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186116543 1064 "In the last example, we approximated the binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim B(50,0.6)\n$$\n\nusing a normal random variable\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12),\n$$\n\nand we arrived at the approximation\n\n$$\nP(X=33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796.\n$$\n\nIf you were to use a software package to evaluate this approximation, you might get an answer like\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0791.\n$$\n\nNotice that there is a difference of around $0.0005$ between the two answers.\n\nThere is a reason for this discrepancy. When we calculated that $P(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796,$ we rounded our $z$-values two decimal places and then used a lookup table to find $\\Phi(z)$ for those rounded values. As a result, we introduced some small rounding errors. \n\nTypically, the rounding errors are small, so we will continue to work with a table of values. However, it's worth bearing this in mind if you're using a software package or calculator to compute $\\Phi(z).$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186116546 1064 "According to the so-called **central limit theorem**, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) random variables, then\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is \"sufficiently large,\" and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nNow, if $X\\sim B(n,p)$, then $X$ can be thought of as a sum of $n$ I.I.D Bernoulli random variables\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)\n$$\n\nwhere the PMF of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n$$\n{p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1 \\\\ 1 - p,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2 = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ into our normal approximation for $X$, we get\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\left(np, np(1-p)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe \"sufficiently large\" condition on $n$ is taken care of by stipulating that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$:\n\n- When $p\\approx 0.5$, then $n\\approx 10$ is sufficiently large for the normal approximation to be applied.\n- However, if $p$ is closer to zero or one, then the distribution of $X$ is not symmetric, and consequently, we need $n$ to be much larger for the central limit theorem to be valid." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186116546 1064 "According to the so-called **central limit theorem**, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) random variables, then\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is \"sufficiently large,\" and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X\\sim B(n,p),$ then $X$ can be thought of as a sum of $n$ I.I.D Bernoulli random variables \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p),\n$$\n\nwhere the PMF of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n![](images/13/134fcb82e69ef8d5fc1e63353f3b28c036bda08176918bcd166ebc52826854c6.svg)\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2 = p(1-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ into our normal approximation for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\left(np, np(1-p)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe \"sufficiently large\" condition on $n$ is taken care of by stipulating that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- When $p\\approx 0.5,$ then $n\\approx 10$ is sufficiently large for the normal approximation to be applied.\n\n- However, if $p$ is closer to zero or one, then the distribution of $X$ is not symmetric, and consequently, we need $n$ to be much larger for the central limit theorem to be valid." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186119740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495167 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186119741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495114 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186119741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495157 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186119742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495131 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186119842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495153 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186119848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495078 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186132954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132955 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186132954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132957 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186132954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132958 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186132954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494432 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186132954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494618 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186132954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494792 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186132954 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494618 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133538 1074 "The price before taxes of the turkey is $N$ dollars.\n\nThe amount of tax is $7\\%$ of the price of the turkey, or $0.07N$.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost of the turkey after tax is:\n\n$$\nN+0.07N\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186133538 1074 "The price before taxes of the turkey is $N$ dollars.\n\nThe amount of tax is $7\\%$ of the price of the turkey, or $0.07N.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost of the turkey after tax is: \n\n$$\nN+0.07N\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133673 1071 "The taxi fare, in dollars, charged by a particular cab company is given by the expression\n$6m+8$,\nwhere $m$ is the number of miles traveled. What do the constant term and the coefficient of $m$ represent in this context?" 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186133673 1071 "The taxi fare, in dollars, charged by a particular cab company is given by the expression \n\n$$\n6m+8,\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the number of miles traveled. What do the constant term and the coefficient of $m$ represent 