d the studio charges an fee of $\\[math]55$ per hour, then the cost for $h$ hours must be $55h.$ \n\nHowever, there is also a fixed fee for recording an album. This fee is $\\[/math]70.$ Consequently, the constant term of the expression should be $70.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost of recording an album is:\n\n$$\n55 h+70\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133675 1071 "In a particular fast-food restaurant, a standard lunch consists of $2$ hot dogs for $H$ dollars each, $8$ potato wedges for $W$ dollars each, and $1$ drink $D$ dollars. Monica went to the fast-food restaurant to pick up lunch for her family, and the total bill was\n\n$$\n5(2H + 8W + D)\n$$\n\ndollars. \nWhat does the factor $5$ mean in this context?" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133675 1071 "In a particular fast-food restaurant, a standard lunch consists of $2$ hot dogs for $H$ dollars each, $8$ potato wedges for $W$ dollars each, and $1$ drink $D$ dollars. Monica went to the fast-food restaurant to pick up lunch for her family, and the total bill was\n$5(2H + 8W + D)$\ndollars.\nWhat does the factor $5$ mean in this context?" 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186133675 1074 "Two hot dogs cost $2H$ dollars, eight potato wedges cost $8W$ dollars, and a drink costs $D$ dollars. Thus, each standard lunch costs\n$2H + 8W + D$\ndollars. Monica's bill comes to\n$5(2H + 8W + D)$\ndollars, which is $5$ times the cost of a single standard lunch. Thus, the factor $5$ must be the total number of standard lunches that Monica purchased." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186133675 1074 "Two hot dogs cost $2H$ dollars, eight potato wedges cost $8W$ dollars, and a drink costs $D$ dollars. Thus, each standard lunch costs \n\n$$\n2H + 8W + D\n$$\n\ndollars. Monica's bill comes to \n\n$$\n5(2H + 8W + D)\n$$\n\ndollars, which is $5$ times the cost of a single standard lunch. Thus, the factor $5$ must be the total number of standard lunches that Monica purchased." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495077 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495117 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495120 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495057 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495070 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495088 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495169 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495139 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495112 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495140 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495141 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495172 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495145 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495162 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495056 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495065 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186133965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495109 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186134048 1050 420.0 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186134070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130576 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186134072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130588 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186134486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186134486 1074 "If $n$ is the number of toppings, and the pizzeria charges $$3$ per topping, then the cost for $n$ toppings must be $3n.$ \n\nHowever, there is also a fixed fee for the cheese pizza. This fee is $$15.$ Consequently, the constant term of the expression should be $15.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost (in dollars) of a pizza with $n$ toppings is:\n\n$$\n3n+15\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186135012 1064 "The expression below gives the cost, in dollars, of sending a letter to Spain:\n$2w + 1$\nHere, $w$ is the letter's weight in ounces.\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\:{\\color{blue}2}\\!\\!\\!{\\underset{\\underset{\\Large\\textrm{weight}}{\\large\\uparrow}}{w}}\\!\\!\\!+{\\color{red}1}\\:}^{\\large\\textrm{total cost}}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $w$ is the cost for each additional ounce of weight.\n\nTo illustrate:\n\n- If $w=1$ (we have a $1$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]3$.\n- If $w=2$ (we have a $2$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 2 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[/math]5$.\n- If $w=3$ (we have a $3$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 3 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]7$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The number $\\color{blue}2$ in this expression represents a *unit rate.* Each time we increase the letter's weight by $1$ ounce, the cost increases by $\\color{blue}\\[/math]2$.\n- There is also a **base fee** of $\\color{red}\\[math]1$, which does not depend on the weight of the letter. We can think of this as the cost of sending a zero-ounce letter." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186135012 1064 "The expression below gives the cost, in dollars, of sending a letter to Spain: \n\n$$\n2w + 1\n$$\n\nHere, $w$ is the letter's weight in ounces.\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\:{\\color{blue}2}\\!\\!\\!{\\underset{\\underset{\\Large\\textrm{weight}}{\\large\\uparrow}}{w}}\\!\\!\\!+{\\color{red}1}\\:}^{\\large\\textrm{total cost}}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $w$ is the cost for each additional ounce of weight.\n\nTo illustrate:\n\n- If $w=1$ (we have a $1$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]3.$\n\n- If $w=2$ (we have a $2$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 2 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[/math]5.$\n\n- If $w=3$ (we have a $3$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 3 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]7.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The number $\\color{blue}2$ in this expression represents a *unit rate.* Each time we increase the letter's weight by $1$ ounce, the cost increases by $\\color{blue}\\[/math]2$.\n\n- There is also a **base fee** of $\\color{red}\\[math]1$, which does not depend on the weight of the letter. We can think of this as the cost of sending a zero-ounce letter." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186135149 1064 "In many countries, a **sales tax** is charged whenever an individual or company makes a purchase. The tax is usually based on a certain percentage of the sale price.\n\nFor example, suppose a person purchases a bicycle for $\\[math] 200$, and there is a sales tax of $5\\%$. How much is this tax, and what is the total cost of the bicycle?\n\nTo determine the amount of the tax the person must pay, we multiply $200 \\times 5\\%$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTax &= \\[/math]200 \\cdot 5% \\\\\n&= \\[math]200 \\cdot 0.05 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the purchaser must pay a $\\[math]10$ sales tax. This means that the total amount the person needs to pay for the bicycle is\n\n$$\n\\[/math]200 + \\[math]10 = \\[/math]210\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186135149 1064 "In many countries, a **sales tax** is charged whenever an individual or company makes a purchase. The tax is usually based on a certain percentage of the sale price.\n\nFor example, suppose a person purchases a bicycle for $\\[math] 200,$ and there is a sales tax of $5\\%.$ How much is this tax, and what is the total cost of the bicycle?\n\nTo determine the amount of the tax the person must pay, we multiply $200 \\times 5\\%$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tax & =\\text{\\[}/math]200\\times 5% \\\\  & =\\text{\\[}math]200\\times 0.05 \\\\  & =\\text{\\[}/math]10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the purchaser must pay a $\\[math]10$ sales tax. This means that the total amount the person needs to pay for the bicycle is\n\n$$\n\\[/math]200 + \\[math]10 = \\[/math]210.\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138083 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=8 \\\\ 2x-y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138083 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n${x + 2y = 8; 2x - y = 6$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186138083 1074 "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2)$." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186138083 1074 "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines. \n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2).$ Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2).$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138084 1074 "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186138084 1074 "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400).$ So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495121 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495142 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138429 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495118 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138429 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495144 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186138586 1050 420.0 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186139667 1064 "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-4 \\\\ 6x-y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/59/59f16d57e74fd94fdab464eca22cea1271b19a3b78114446e31343cfabd15a12.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6).$\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations. \n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & \\,=\\,-4 \\\\ (2)-(6) & =-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & \\,=\\,6 \\\\ 6(2)-6 & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186139667 1064 "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n{x - y =-4; 6x - y = 6\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/ee/ee391c2910264abc2962d0c5ec3d971eb7b0b818b5bd59af33fc16ab9e931d11.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6)$.\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations.\n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -4 \\\\\n(2) - (6) &= -4 \\\\\n- 4 &= -4✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - y &= 6 \\\\\n6(2) - 6 &= 6 \\\\\n6 &= 6✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186147615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495110 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186152220 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495073 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186152220 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495107 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186152221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495165 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186152222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495081 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186159692 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159694 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186159728 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159733 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161265 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161266 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161267 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161268 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161269 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494338 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494410 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494540 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494635 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186161264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494678 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186161264 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494678 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495170 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162125 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495160 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162130 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495171 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162131 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495063 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162415 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n\n$F(9, 10) = 0.65, F(6, 10) = 0.31, F(3, 10) = 0.12$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {3, 6, 9, 12}, S_{Y} = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}$.\n\nFind $P(3 < X \\le 9, Y \\le 10)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186162415 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(9,10)=0.65,\\,F(6,10)=0.31,\\,F(3,10)=0.12,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(3<X\\le 9,Y\\le 10).$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162415 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/77/7790a013e37bb86c00b44b1c022d6597f9bbdd57616c48e7ee7dfe8ddf512c63.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/37/379f0690fdbe92b7fb8f66cce4f12b214a23c6299fb8df71edf56d37fc0387fe.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(9,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,10)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/90a6730747b59a9886acbdf21880ca6a7ec3266142522ea5b32f07090963a98d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X\\le 9,Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(9,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 9,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 10)}} \\\\  & =F(9,10)-F(3,10) \\\\  & =0.65-0.12 \\\\  & =0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162421 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n\n$F(7, 1) = \\frac{9}{16}, F(7,-2) = \\frac{1}{4}, F(3, 1) = \\frac{1}{8}, F(3,-2) = \\frac{1}{16}$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}, S_{Y} = {-2,-1, 0, 1, 2}$.\n\nFind $P(5 \\le X \\le 8,-1 \\le Y \\le 1)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186162421 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(7,1)=\\frac{9}{16},\\,F(7,-2)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,F(3,1)=\\frac{1}{8},\\,F(3,-2)=\\frac{1}{16},\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(5\\le X\\le 8,-1\\le Y\\le 1).$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162421 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebff161899c6f61af3dffc1a5a0069de14bafd9b138293a945138e2951d066d.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(7,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(3,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/e2/e25278ed6d9712d8d2d2cabda5d1d0dbbf10aa8193f1342e7b4b66b26f9154e3.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(7,-2)$ and $(3,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 8,-1\\le Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{bottom-right corner at }(7,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-left corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{bottom-left corner at }(3,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(7,1)-F(7,-2)-F(3,1)+F(3,-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161174 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186162662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162662 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x ∈ R^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-10$ of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186162662 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186162662 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-10.$ Computing $A-(-10)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+10I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]+10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 25 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-5{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{26}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186198373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495059 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186198373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495100 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186229132 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495074 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186229132 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495161 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186231428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495094 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186307570 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494655 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186307570 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306852 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186307570 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494655 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186307741 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494519 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186307741 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305469 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186307741 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494519 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186307958 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494533 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186307958 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306955 13194139535319 17592186045417 false] [17592186307958 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494533 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186308718 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186308718 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\be