th Unit Prices" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495275 1033 6818 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495275 1035 #uuid "04654553-f8da-5ec9-a27d-ee5bae447da5" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495276 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495041 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495276 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495044 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495276 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495045 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495276 1031 "Finding Unit Prices" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495276 1033 2555 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495276 1035 #uuid "cef7c109-a883-5959-842c-c3cb8202ae04" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495277 1064 "Now, suppose that installing a fence costs $\\[math]360$ per yard. What would be the unit price per foot?\n\nFirst, notice that there are $3$ feet in $1$ yard.  \n\nTo find the unit price per foot, we *divide* the price per yard by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n360 \\div 3 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, installing the fence costs $\\[/math]120$ per foot.\n\nIn reverse, if we know that installing a fence costs $\\[math]120$ per foot, to find the unit price per yard, we would need to *multiply* the price per foot by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n120 \\times 3 = 360\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495277 1065 15094 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495277 1066 #uuid "15c0e757-69bb-5bfd-a417-6de7c79fa333" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495277 1067 "Unit Prices With Units Conversions" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495278 1064 "For every unit price in \"dollars per item\", there is also a **reciprocal unit price** in \"items per dollar\".\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells fabric for $\\[math]2$ per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\textrm{dollars per yard} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}  \\,\\textrm{yards per dollar} .\n$$\n\nSo, if we have $\\[/math]1,$ then we can buy $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a yard of fabric." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495278 1065 3106 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495278 1066 #uuid "18f05709-44a6-59bc-962c-dbf491ad7501" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495278 1067 "Reciprocal Unit Prices" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495279 1064 "A **unit price** is the price of a single item. To compute a unit price, we divide the total cost of a group of items by the number of items in the group.\n\nFor example, suppose a store sells a pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12.$ \n\nTo find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of shirts:\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3  = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[/math]4$ per shirt." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495279 1065 3104 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495279 1066 #uuid "1e8ab0cc-4a5e-57ca-a168-f89d110ff113" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495279 1067 "Introduction" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495280 1064 "When we compare prices, it is often helpful to know the *unit price*. Knowing it helps us:\n\n- compare different products,\n\n- determine the total cost for a given amount,\n\n- decide which option is the better deal.\n\nFor example, suppose Lena wants to buy some apples. The price of apples at a nearby market is $\\[math]1.20/\\mathrm{lb}.$ How much would Lena pay for $4$ pounds of apples?\n\nThe unit price is $\\[/math]1.20$ per pound.  \n\nSince Lena is buying $4$ pounds, we multiply the unit price by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.20\\times 4 & =4.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lena would pay $\\[math]4.80$ for $4$ pounds of apples.\n\nWhenever we know the unit price and the quantity, we can find the total cost by multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Unit Price} \\times \\text{Quantity}.\n$$" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495280 1065 15086 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495280 1066 #uuid "55a58a08-8164-5352-9b75-43c62ba6ecc5" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495280 1067 "Introduction" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495281 1064 "Unit prices can be used to quantify how good of a deal a particular purchase is.\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells two packs of shirts:\n\n- $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12$\n\n- $5$ shirts for $\\[/math]15$\n\nTo determine which pack is the better deal, we can compare their unit prices.\n\n- For the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- For the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n15 \\div 5 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]3.$\n\nNow we interpret the unit prices in the context of the problem.\n\n- In the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- In the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]3.$\n\nBased on the above, we conclude that the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15$ is the better deal because it has a lower unit price (meaning that each shirt costs less)." 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495281 1065 15097 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495281 1066 #uuid "be64d01c-4eb7-5332-b26c-97583b224a56" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true] [17592186495281 1067 "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices" 13194139535319 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-09d4bb8f-af12-534d-8b4d-e6041622fc03" 17592186494299 "activity-13caabb2-fccc-503a-88a9-84f1640d4d82" 17592186494300 "activity-24b39a23-bcdc-53b3-a773-ff2a2a0400ae" 17592186494301 "activity-28e4707c-4172-5804-bb5b-31ce59766c25" 17592186494302 "activity-3fc4b6f4-448c-556a-9808-6a0a8e96a629" 17592186494303 "activity-45da9bbc-1512-5651-8f30-bc8c978f6a42" 17592186494304 "activity-4ad6406f-28ca-5b57-94e6-4b4cc6fef04e" 17592186494305 "activity-6aa5b166-dd8e-5ca2-8b7d-1419def83709